----------------------------\nMachine learning - Latest research and news | Nature (https://www.nature.com/subjects/machine-learning)\nciteturn779view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn777view1\",\"lineno\":15}); Total lines: 259\nL0:   * cite0†View all journals L1:   * cite1†Saved research L2:   * cite2†Search L3:   * cite3†Account†my-profile.springernature.com cite4†Log in†idp.nature.com L4: # Machine learning articles from across Nature Portfolio\nL5: \nL6:   * cite5†Atom L7:   * cite6†RSS Feed L8: \nL9: [Button: Definition]\nL10: \nL11: Machine learning is the ability of a machine to improve its performance based on previous results. Machine learning methods enable computers to learn without being explicitly programmed and have multiple applications, for example, in the improvement of data mining algorithms.\nL12: \nL13: ## Featured\nL14: \nL15:   * cite7†Image†media.springernature.com L16: ### cite8†When pathology segmentation learns to listen L17: \nL18: A natural-language-guided pathology segmentation model is developed to link pathological language with pathology image content to produce semantic masks, offering a path toward computational pathology systems that are more flexible, interpretable and aligned with human expertise.\nL19: \nL20:     * Wei Shen\nL21: \nL22: News & Views 10 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature Computational Science L23: \nL24: P: 1-2\nL25: \nL26:   * cite10†Image†media.springernature.com L27: ### cite11†Steering machine reasoning with brain signals L28: \nL29: Representational alignment can reveal similarities between human brain activity and language models. Work now demonstrates that it can also guide learning, improving the reliability of artificial reasoning.\nL30: \nL31:     * Changde Du\nL32:     * Huiguang He\nL33: \nL34: News & Views 01 Sept 2026 cite12†Nature Machine Intelligence L35: \nL36: P: 1-2\nL37: \nL38:   * cite13†Image†media.springernature.com L39: ### cite14†A patient’s longitudinal history reconstructs their unmeasured molecular profile L40: \nL41: Most patients leave the clinic with only a few of the clinical measurements needed to tell the full story of their health. PULSE is a new artificial intelligence (AI) framework that fills in the blanks, using a patient’s own history to predict what was never directly measured.\nL42: \nL43:     * Stephanie Tanis\nL44:     * Samantha Lopez Alvarez\nL45:     * Natalie R. Davidson\nL46: \nL47: News & Views 26 Aug 2026 cite9†Nature Computational Science L48: P: 1-2\nL49: ## Latest Research and Reviews\nL50: \nL51:   * ### cite15†On-demand design of controlled-release systems using an expert-mimic AI framework L52: \nL53: Here the authors develop an AI framework called E-MAF that, starting from a desired release profile, couples process-aware prediction with expert-mimic evolutionary search to generate interpretable, lab-ready formulations for controlled release systems.\nL54: \nL55:     * Ying Qin\nL56:     * Rushuang Zhou\nL57:     * Yufei Xia\nL58: \nL59: Research Open Access 15 Sept 2026 cite16†Nature Communications L60:   * ### cite17†scTransMIL bridges patient-level disease states and single-cell transcriptomics for cancer screening and heterogeneity inference L61: \nL62: Reliably linking patient-level cancer phenotypes to single-cell transcriptomes re-mains challenging due to limited cell-level labels. Here, the authors develop scTransMIL, a transformer-based multi-instance learning framework that identifies cancer states, cancer subtypes, and potential cancer biomarkers with good performance across datasets.\nL63:     * Zhenchao Tang\nL64:     * Fang Wang\nL65:     * Jianhua Yao\nL66: \nL67: Research Open Access 14 Sept 2026 cite16†Nature Communications L68: \nL69:   * ### cite18†A clinically-oriented foundation model for intraoperative pathology L70: \nL71: CRISP, a vision-based pathology foundation model developed exclusively from frozen section slides, supports treatment decision-making throughout the surgical workflow with superior performance to current foundation models and extensive validation, including in a prospective cohort.\nL72:     * Zihan Zhao\nL73:     * Fengtao Zhou\nL74:     * Muyan Cai\nL75: \nL76: Research Open Access 10 Sept 2026 cite19†Nature Medicine L77: \nL78: P: 1\nL79: \nL80:   * cite20†Image†media.springernature.com L81: ### cite21†Segment anything in pathology images with natural language L82: \nL83: This Resource introduces PathSegmentor—a foundation model that segments 160 pathological categories via natural language prompts, generalizes across datasets and clinical cohorts, and enables scalable, interpretable pathology image analysis.\nL84: \nL85:     * Zhixuan Chen\nL86:     * Junlin Hou\nL87:     * Hao Chen\nL88: \nL89: Research 10 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature Computational Science L90: \nL91: P: 1-15\nL92:   * ### cite22†Explainable data generation from observed small samples by matrix tri-factorization L93: \nL94: Here the authors propose an explainable data generation approach called EDG that operates under extreme sample scarcity by exploiting inherent low-dimensional principles contained in the observed high-dimensional data via matrix tri-factorization.\nL95: \nL96:     * Bin Zhang\nL97:     * Peng Tao\nL98:     * Luonan Chen\nL99: \nL100: Research Open Access 09 Sept 2026 cite16†Nature Communications L101:   * ### cite23†Reducing cost of fluorescent microscopy through dye exclusion, distillation techniques, and deep learning L102: \nL103:     * Adriana Borowa\nL104:     * Bartosz Zieliński\nL105:     * Dawid Rymarczyk\nL106: \nL107: Research Open Access 09 Sept 2026 cite24†Scientific Reports L108: \nL109: cite25†All Research & Reviews L110: ## News and Comment\nL111: \nL112:   * cite26†Image†media.springernature.com L113: \nL114: ### cite27†‘Multifunctional’ brain implant translates speech and gestures in real time L115: \nL116: A neural device uses artificial intelligence to read brain activity for intended words and gestures simultaneously.\nL117: \nL118:     * Miryam Naddaf\nL119: \nL120: News 14 Sept 2026 cite28†Nature L121: \nL122:   * cite29†Image†media.springernature.com L123: ### cite30†Drug firms’ secret data supercharge AI protein models L124: \nL125: An AI system trained on more than 20,000 protein structures from pharmaceutical companies outperforms AlphaFold-like models that use only public data.\nL126: \nL127:     * Ewen Callaway\nL128: \nL129: News 14 Sept 2026 cite28†Nature L130: \nL131:   * cite31†Image†media.springernature.com L132: ### cite32†Briefing Chat: The Bunsen burner myth that turns out to be just hot air L133: \nL134: Nature staff discuss an AI tool that can assess the impact of 9 billion mutations — plus, how the Bunsen burner’s reputation for sterilization might be based on tradition alone.\nL135: \nL136:     * Shamini Bundell\nL137:     * Nick Petrić Howe\nL138: \nL139: News 11 Sept 2026 cite28†Nature L140: \nL141:   * cite7†Image†media.springernature.com L142: ### cite8†When pathology segmentation learns to listen L143: \nL144: A natural-language-guided pathology segmentation model is developed to link pathological language with pathology image content to produce semantic masks, offering a path toward computational pathology systems that are more flexible, interpretable and aligned with human expertise.\nL145: \nL146:     * Wei Shen\nL147: \nL148: News & Views 10 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature Computational Science L149: \nL150: P: 1-2\nL151: \nL152:   * cite33†Image†media.springernature.com L153: ### cite34†Use AI as a sparring partner, not an oracle L154: \nL155: As artificial-intelligence tools become crucial collaborators in science, universities must teach students to challenge such systems and assess their ability to do so.\nL156: \nL157:     * Marc Schmitt\nL158: \nL159: Comments & Opinion 09 Sept 2026 cite28†Nature L160: \nL161: Volume: 657, P: 575\nL162: \nL163:   * cite35†Image†media.springernature.com L164: ### cite36†AI model predicts which breast-cancer drugs work best L165: \nL166: Model trained on millions of protein measurements can gauge drug effectiveness in tissue samples taken from people with triple-negative breast cancer.\nL167: \nL168:     * Miryam Naddaf\nL169: \nL170: News 09 Sept 2026 cite28†Nature L171: \nL172: cite37†All News & Comment L173: \nL174: ## Search\nL175: \nL176: Search articles by subject, keyword or author\nL177: \nL178: [Input]\nL179: \nL180: Show results from [Select]\nL181: \nL182: [Button: Search]\nL183: \nL184: cite38†Advanced search L185: ### Quick links\nL186: \nL187:   * cite39†Explore articles by subject L188:   * cite40†Find a job L189:   * cite41†Guide to authors L190:   * cite42†Editorial policies L191: \nL192: Nature.com\nL193: \nL194: ## nature.com footer links\nL195: \nL196: ### About Nature Portfolio\nL197: \nL198:   * cite43†About us L199:   * cite44†Press releases L200:   * cite45†Press office†press.nature.com L201:   * cite46†Contact us†support.nature.com L202: \nL203: ### Discover content\nL204: \nL205:   * cite0†Journals A-Z L206:   * cite39†Articles by subject L207:   * cite47†protocols.io†www.protocols.io L208:   * cite48†Nature Index†www.natureindex.com L209: ### Publishing policies\nL210: \nL211:   * cite49†Nature portfolio policies L212:   * cite50†Open access L213: \nL214: ### Author & Researcher services\nL215: \nL216:   * cite51†Reprints & permissions L217:   * cite52†Research data†www.springernature.com L218:   * cite53†Language editing†authorservices.springernature.com L219:   * cite54†Scientific editing†authorservices.springernature.com L220:   * cite55†Nature Masterclasses†masterclasses.nature.com L221:   * cite56†Research Solutions†solutions.springernature.com L222: ### Libraries & institutions\nL223: \nL224:   * cite57†Librari